00:02:30.012 --> 00:02:31.220
DANIEL MOLLURA: Hi, everyone.

00:02:31.220 --> 00:02:32.700
I'm Dan Mollura.

00:02:32.700 --> 00:02:36.100
I'm the CEO of
Rad-Aid International.

00:02:36.100 --> 00:02:37.960
So we're a nonprofit
organization

00:02:37.960 --> 00:02:41.470
that brings radiology
to low resource regions,

00:02:41.470 --> 00:02:43.330
developing countries.

00:02:43.330 --> 00:02:47.800
We were founded in 2008 to
address the problem that

00:02:47.800 --> 00:02:49.600
radiology is missing
in about half

00:02:49.600 --> 00:02:51.800
to two thirds of the world.

00:02:51.800 --> 00:02:54.190
That means that all
the medical images that

00:02:54.190 --> 00:02:56.840
are so vital for diagnosing
and treating disease

00:02:56.840 --> 00:03:02.380
are mostly absent in these low
and middle-income countries.

00:03:02.380 --> 00:03:05.740
We have about 11,000 volunteers
that go to these countries

00:03:05.740 --> 00:03:07.510
to build radiology capacities.

00:03:07.510 --> 00:03:11.770
So that might mean donating
equipment, doing trainings,

00:03:11.770 --> 00:03:15.550
education for health personnel
to use medical imaging in a way

00:03:15.550 --> 00:03:18.760
that can benefit the patients.

00:03:18.760 --> 00:03:21.130
A large part of what
we do now is also

00:03:21.130 --> 00:03:24.400
donating the IT
infrastructure and bringing

00:03:24.400 --> 00:03:28.720
in PACS and other IT
infrastructures that

00:03:28.720 --> 00:03:31.150
are vital for bringing the
images from the scanners

00:03:31.150 --> 00:03:35.170
over to the workstations so
that they can be interpreted,

00:03:35.170 --> 00:03:37.420
which, of course, raises
the big opportunity

00:03:37.420 --> 00:03:40.840
for long-term artificial
intelligence development

00:03:40.840 --> 00:03:44.000
and other advanced
IT infrastructures.

00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:45.547
So it's great work
that we love doing

00:03:45.547 --> 00:03:46.630
and it's great to be here.

00:03:46.630 --> 00:03:48.330
Thanks.

00:03:48.330 --> 00:03:50.080
RAFAEL DE CASTRO
FIGUEROA: Hello everyone.

00:03:50.080 --> 00:03:52.650
I'm Rafael, co-founder
and CEO of Portal.

00:03:52.650 --> 00:03:55.750
We are a telediagnostic
platform that connects clinics,

00:03:55.750 --> 00:03:58.850
hospitals, and governments.

00:03:58.850 --> 00:04:01.840
Our goal is to provide access
to quality health care,

00:04:01.840 --> 00:04:03.630
especially in remote areas.

00:04:03.630 --> 00:04:05.610
So we develop a
technology that integrates

00:04:05.610 --> 00:04:07.030
with medical devices.

00:04:07.030 --> 00:04:10.410
So the nurse, usually, or a
general physician, do the exam,

00:04:10.410 --> 00:04:12.750
data is automatically
transferred to the cloud,

00:04:12.750 --> 00:04:15.510
and the medical specialists like
a neurologist or cardiologist

00:04:15.510 --> 00:04:17.160
makes the diagnostic online.

00:04:17.160 --> 00:04:19.709
This result is available
in the same day

00:04:19.709 --> 00:04:22.350
to our network of
clients of 500 hospitals

00:04:22.350 --> 00:04:25.550
and 300 cities in
Brazil and Africa.

00:04:25.550 --> 00:04:28.980
Through our proprietary data set
of about 30 million patients,

00:04:28.980 --> 00:04:31.770
we train machine learning
models running in TensorFlow

00:04:31.770 --> 00:04:34.770
to automatically detect
diseases in these exams.

00:04:34.770 --> 00:04:37.080
So whenever the ML
model detects a disease,

00:04:37.080 --> 00:04:38.940
it assigns a risk
score and moves it up

00:04:38.940 --> 00:04:40.150
in the queue to emergencies.

00:04:40.150 --> 00:04:43.410
So this is how we do such a
large scale because we use ML

00:04:43.410 --> 00:04:45.260
to prioritize their emergence.

00:04:45.260 --> 00:04:48.240
So the doctors do the
diagnostics on top of this.

00:04:48.240 --> 00:04:50.370
Our goal here at Next,
we're looking for partners

00:04:50.370 --> 00:04:53.130
to help in this global
expansion and in our mission

00:04:53.130 --> 00:04:55.620
to provide universal
access to health care.

00:04:55.620 --> 00:04:56.983
Thank you.

00:04:56.983 --> 00:04:58.150
ARIE MEIR: Thank you, folks.

00:04:58.150 --> 00:05:01.180
So this has been, actually,
a personal dream of mine

00:05:01.180 --> 00:05:04.240
to bring people from solving
a very similar challenge

00:05:04.240 --> 00:05:06.310
in different
geographies, and kind

00:05:06.310 --> 00:05:10.840
of trying to find what's common
and what's idiosyncratic.

00:05:10.840 --> 00:05:14.030
In my mind I was calling it
the Medical Imaging Olympics.

00:05:14.030 --> 00:05:16.970
We have a representative from
four different geographies.

00:05:16.970 --> 00:05:19.450
So it sounds like
the problem we're all

00:05:19.450 --> 00:05:21.220
trying to approach,
your organizations,

00:05:21.220 --> 00:05:24.580
is increasing access
to quality care.

00:05:24.580 --> 00:05:26.590
So you have expertise
in one place

00:05:26.590 --> 00:05:29.916
and you have the people
who need to benefit from it

00:05:29.916 --> 00:05:31.480
in a different place.

00:05:31.480 --> 00:05:33.760
What do you find idiosyncratic?

00:05:33.760 --> 00:05:35.777
What's kind of unique
from your observation

00:05:35.777 --> 00:05:37.360
and your conversations
with colleagues

00:05:37.360 --> 00:05:40.000
from other parts of
the world that might

00:05:40.000 --> 00:05:41.862
be unique to your geography?

00:05:41.862 --> 00:05:43.570
We don't have necessarily
to go by order.

00:05:43.570 --> 00:05:46.670
Whoever feels like they
have something, jump in.

00:05:46.670 --> 00:05:47.920
RAFAEL DE CASTRO FIGUEROA: OK.

00:05:47.920 --> 00:05:51.070
So what I see in
similarities and difference,

00:05:51.070 --> 00:05:52.930
first, is the matter
of legislation.

00:05:52.930 --> 00:05:56.440
I believe each of us has a
different scenario to operate.

00:05:56.440 --> 00:05:59.417
And especially, Dan, when
people say Africa is not Africa.

00:05:59.417 --> 00:06:01.000
There are a lot of
different countries

00:06:01.000 --> 00:06:02.570
with different regulation.

00:06:02.570 --> 00:06:05.690
We think in the case of the
US, there's different states.

00:06:05.690 --> 00:06:08.895
So, for example, in Brazil, we
have one legislation's federal,

00:06:08.895 --> 00:06:10.270
and with this
legislation, we can

00:06:10.270 --> 00:06:13.180
do diagnostics between the
states with no problem.

00:06:13.180 --> 00:06:15.100
Here in the US, there
are several states

00:06:15.100 --> 00:06:17.500
that doesn't allow the
license for the other states,

00:06:17.500 --> 00:06:20.320
so you need to hire
local doctors, which

00:06:20.320 --> 00:06:22.870
is one of the barriers of the
adoption of telediagnostics

00:06:22.870 --> 00:06:23.960
here.

00:06:23.960 --> 00:06:27.640
And also in terms
of technology, I'm

00:06:27.640 --> 00:06:30.880
surprised to see that what I
thought was a problem in Brazil

00:06:30.880 --> 00:06:33.820
in emerging markets, for
example, interoperability,

00:06:33.820 --> 00:06:35.620
that the systems don't
talk between them,

00:06:35.620 --> 00:06:38.200
and the devices also
have a lot of problems

00:06:38.200 --> 00:06:42.520
with proprietary files, we're
seeing similar issues happen

00:06:42.520 --> 00:06:45.790
in the US and Europe as well
for different reasons, maybe.

00:06:45.790 --> 00:06:49.120
Like very big vendors that
don't want to integrate,

00:06:49.120 --> 00:06:51.850
as opposed in
Brazil, we have a lot

00:06:51.850 --> 00:06:55.120
of smaller vendors, which
do want to integrate.

00:06:55.120 --> 00:06:58.840
So I see as a matter
of technical challenge,

00:06:58.840 --> 00:07:01.160
most emerging markets
as there are here,

00:07:01.160 --> 00:07:04.180
and some places in Europe as
more of a political challenge

00:07:04.180 --> 00:07:06.560
between these big players.

00:07:06.560 --> 00:07:09.910
But it's nice to see that we
have a lot of similarities

00:07:09.910 --> 00:07:11.660
between the way that we work.

00:07:11.660 --> 00:07:13.450
And this is one of
the reasons that we

00:07:13.450 --> 00:07:17.170
are using GCP, because
there's some infrastructure

00:07:17.170 --> 00:07:20.260
challenges that is
similar between us,

00:07:20.260 --> 00:07:23.363
and Google is helping
us to solve them.

00:07:23.363 --> 00:07:25.030
DANIEL MOLLURA: Yeah,
so Rafael's right.

00:07:25.030 --> 00:07:27.310
There's a lot of
heterogeneity, diversity

00:07:27.310 --> 00:07:28.910
across these locations.

00:07:28.910 --> 00:07:33.140
Rad-Aid works in 30 countries
and about 55 hospitals.

00:07:33.140 --> 00:07:38.110
So we see a lot of differences
across these locations.

00:07:38.110 --> 00:07:39.790
I think the big thing
that we're finding

00:07:39.790 --> 00:07:42.580
that's idiosyncratic and
important to point out

00:07:42.580 --> 00:07:47.140
is that with all this technology
development and the algorithms

00:07:47.140 --> 00:07:50.410
and what's coming for the future
of artificial intelligence

00:07:50.410 --> 00:07:53.560
in health care and
medicine, Rad-Aid

00:07:53.560 --> 00:07:55.630
is here to empower the user.

00:07:55.630 --> 00:07:59.950
There is still a gap between the
user, which is the health care

00:07:59.950 --> 00:08:04.300
professional, and the great
technologies that are capable.

00:08:04.300 --> 00:08:06.190
So by empowering the
user, we're talking

00:08:06.190 --> 00:08:08.350
about really retraining
health care workers

00:08:08.350 --> 00:08:11.050
to think a little differently
in their clinical practice

00:08:11.050 --> 00:08:15.620
and how they adopt technologies
into their daily work.

00:08:15.620 --> 00:08:18.430
And that's something we can
do by integrating things

00:08:18.430 --> 00:08:22.240
like cloud and AI into the
practice of health care.

00:08:22.240 --> 00:08:23.920
And we're finding
that that's going

00:08:23.920 --> 00:08:28.840
to require a lot of on-site
work and a lot of understanding

00:08:28.840 --> 00:08:32.049
of what the uniqueness
characteristics are

00:08:32.049 --> 00:08:35.049
of these hospitals and how
they work in their cultures

00:08:35.049 --> 00:08:37.240
and appreciating what that
heterogeneity might be,

00:08:37.240 --> 00:08:40.510
but at the same time leveraging
the great technologies that

00:08:40.510 --> 00:08:42.504
are coming from this
kind of context.

00:08:45.340 --> 00:08:47.530
TAKASHI TOCHI: Talking
about a common feature

00:08:47.530 --> 00:08:49.630
of teleradiology,
especially, this

00:08:49.630 --> 00:08:53.750
is very similar
with Dan's Rad-Aid.

00:08:53.750 --> 00:08:57.010
Please let me explain
about our Mongol project.

00:08:57.010 --> 00:08:59.710
Now we're proceeding
a three-year project

00:08:59.710 --> 00:09:01.150
with Mongol doctors.

00:09:01.150 --> 00:09:05.440
Every year we hold a
one-week training seminar

00:09:05.440 --> 00:09:07.600
in our office in
Hiroshima, Japan,

00:09:07.600 --> 00:09:10.430
inviting Mongolian doctors.

00:09:10.430 --> 00:09:14.540
In that seminar, firstly, we
teach them how to [INAUDIBLE]

00:09:14.540 --> 00:09:18.560
interpret, because Mongolian
doctors are still a little bit

00:09:18.560 --> 00:09:22.040
behind in their
diagnostic skills.

00:09:22.040 --> 00:09:25.190
So we teach them how to
read, how to interpret,

00:09:25.190 --> 00:09:30.410
but rather, we focus on teaching
them how to use our system.

00:09:30.410 --> 00:09:35.810
That's because that's a kind
of preparation for elearning.

00:09:35.810 --> 00:09:37.670
Of course, one-week
training seminar,

00:09:37.670 --> 00:09:40.670
that's not enough to
build strong skills.

00:09:40.670 --> 00:09:46.730
So after the seminar is over,
they're back to their hometown,

00:09:46.730 --> 00:09:50.360
and while they're making
diagnosis in their daily job,

00:09:50.360 --> 00:09:53.960
they consult with
us via our system.

00:09:53.960 --> 00:09:57.410
Oh, doc, I think this
tumor is not malignant,

00:09:57.410 --> 00:09:58.950
do you agree or disagree?

00:09:58.950 --> 00:10:00.810
That kind of thing.

00:10:00.810 --> 00:10:03.910
One doctor told me, oh,
this is a ideal platform

00:10:03.910 --> 00:10:05.270
for consultation.

00:10:05.270 --> 00:10:10.750
We can discuss seeing the
same images at one time.

00:10:10.750 --> 00:10:17.160
And we can do that with
doctors all over the world.

00:10:17.160 --> 00:10:23.340
And not only us, but we all can
do that because that's cloud.

00:10:23.340 --> 00:10:29.380
That's cloud's value.

00:10:29.380 --> 00:10:33.890
Teleradiology and
cloud are inseparable.

00:10:33.890 --> 00:10:36.610
Thank you.

00:10:36.610 --> 00:10:40.000
LORENZO RIZZATTO: I believe
that one of the main problems

00:10:40.000 --> 00:10:43.830
is standardization,
as Rafael mentioned.

00:10:43.830 --> 00:10:51.240
And we face really
big complexities

00:10:51.240 --> 00:10:58.180
with collection of data
around the territory,

00:10:58.180 --> 00:11:02.770
but as soon as they reach
the cloud, it's all done.

00:11:02.770 --> 00:11:08.500
And we have no complex
scenario to face.

00:11:08.500 --> 00:11:16.960
And of course, we also
face regulation problems.

00:11:16.960 --> 00:11:26.890
As you know, in Europe, we have
since 2018, a new General Data

00:11:26.890 --> 00:11:31.180
Protection Regulation,
which is really strict.

00:11:31.180 --> 00:11:41.680
And we have to collect
consent from the patients,

00:11:41.680 --> 00:11:48.520
and all the process
must be audited

00:11:48.520 --> 00:11:52.980
and must be transparent.

00:11:52.980 --> 00:11:58.690
So this is made, of course,
easy with Google Cloud,

00:11:58.690 --> 00:12:05.125
since the platform
itself is GDPR-compliant.

00:12:09.313 --> 00:12:10.480
ARIE MEIR: Thank you, folks.

00:12:10.480 --> 00:12:13.280
So one thing I want to point
out that kind of stood out to me

00:12:13.280 --> 00:12:15.320
is one of the
idiosyncrasies I remember

00:12:15.320 --> 00:12:17.480
when first learning
about [INAUDIBLE] work

00:12:17.480 --> 00:12:20.210
is a data point.

00:12:20.210 --> 00:12:24.410
And in the US and in Japan,
you have a comparable number

00:12:24.410 --> 00:12:28.670
of medical imaging
devices, like CTs and MRIs.

00:12:28.670 --> 00:12:32.570
Very similar, maybe
10%, 15% difference.

00:12:32.570 --> 00:12:35.360
But the number of doctors,
radiologists in Japan

00:12:35.360 --> 00:12:37.550
is, I think, seven times less.

00:12:37.550 --> 00:12:40.290
So it creates a huge
scarcity for radiologists.

00:12:40.290 --> 00:12:42.890
So it's an interesting
kind of dynamic

00:12:42.890 --> 00:12:46.460
that makes you realize that, OK,
this research is very valuable.

00:12:46.460 --> 00:12:49.820
I need to optimize
it effectively.

00:12:49.820 --> 00:12:52.940
One thing I want to
ask you, if I ask you,

00:12:52.940 --> 00:12:56.933
how many people have spoken
to a doctor on the phone here?

00:12:56.933 --> 00:12:58.100
The majority of people have.

00:12:58.100 --> 00:13:00.642
So in some sense, that's kind
of a basic telemedicine, right?

00:13:00.642 --> 00:13:02.670
You're talking to
a doctor remotely.

00:13:02.670 --> 00:13:06.050
So the phones have been since
Alexander Bell and the internet

00:13:06.050 --> 00:13:07.490
has been around since the '80s.

00:13:07.490 --> 00:13:10.490
So what has cloud changed?

00:13:10.490 --> 00:13:12.987
Or is it just faster internet?

00:13:12.987 --> 00:13:14.570
How are you thinking
about that piece?

00:13:17.780 --> 00:13:19.770
LORENZO RIZZATTO:
Well, I believe

00:13:19.770 --> 00:13:28.150
that with cloud, the time,
the speed of the process

00:13:28.150 --> 00:13:30.910
is much higher, of course.

00:13:33.610 --> 00:13:41.350
I am in the sector since 20
years, so I saw all the phases.

00:13:41.350 --> 00:13:45.190
For example, for our
teleradiology projects,

00:13:45.190 --> 00:13:49.360
we used to have
our own data center

00:13:49.360 --> 00:13:53.530
and we used to move,
point to point, all

00:13:53.530 --> 00:14:01.480
the [INAUDIBLE] images, so
the biggest projects we had,

00:14:01.480 --> 00:14:07.580
they were mammography screening
teleradiology projects.

00:14:07.580 --> 00:14:15.970
So it was really a big,
big pain to move the images

00:14:15.970 --> 00:14:20.080
from the server to
the single point,

00:14:20.080 --> 00:14:24.850
to a single workstation of the
radiologist that was reporting

00:14:24.850 --> 00:14:26.430
for [INAUDIBLE], for example.

00:14:26.430 --> 00:14:30.400
So it was really a long
process, and many people

00:14:30.400 --> 00:14:35.770
were involved to monitor
all the workflow.

00:14:35.770 --> 00:14:41.900
But now, with the cloud,
it takes some minutes.

00:14:41.900 --> 00:14:46.180
So we collect the images,
we collect the priors

00:14:46.180 --> 00:14:49.060
so the actual study
plus the priors,

00:14:49.060 --> 00:14:53.680
and then they are immediately
available to everyone

00:14:53.680 --> 00:14:58.810
on whatever device
they want to use.

00:14:58.810 --> 00:15:02.140
Of course they are using
multi-monitor workstations.

00:15:02.140 --> 00:15:07.100
So it takes minutes from
acquisition to reporting.

00:15:07.100 --> 00:15:12.130
So this is, in my opinion, one
of the best, the most important

00:15:12.130 --> 00:15:13.500
point.

00:15:13.500 --> 00:15:19.770
And of course there
are less mistakes

00:15:19.770 --> 00:15:27.360
in the process because it's
simplified by technology.

00:15:27.360 --> 00:15:31.520
TAKASHI TOCHI: As
Arie told, in Japan,

00:15:31.520 --> 00:15:35.150
the ratio of
radiologists to scanners,

00:15:35.150 --> 00:15:38.300
that ratio is
ridiculously low in Japan

00:15:38.300 --> 00:15:40.050
comparing with other countries.

00:15:40.050 --> 00:15:43.160
We have many, many scanners,
CT or MRI scanners,

00:15:43.160 --> 00:15:46.820
but the number of radiologists
is strictly limited.

00:15:46.820 --> 00:15:51.170
And last year in Japan,
overlooked cancers

00:15:51.170 --> 00:15:53.870
became a social problem.

00:15:53.870 --> 00:15:57.350
Very famous, large,
respected hospitals,

00:15:57.350 --> 00:16:02.390
announced one after another
that they did overlook.

00:16:02.390 --> 00:16:06.020
And sadly, some resulted
in patients' deaths.

00:16:06.020 --> 00:16:09.150
That gained
nationwide attention.

00:16:09.150 --> 00:16:12.590
And as a result,
as you can tell,

00:16:12.590 --> 00:16:18.370
the result is raised
expectations for teleradiology.

00:16:18.370 --> 00:16:20.400
The radiologists
are short in number.

00:16:20.400 --> 00:16:25.670
So in order to centralize
and make it more efficient,

00:16:25.670 --> 00:16:30.160
teleradiology is
essential, indispensable.

00:16:30.160 --> 00:16:38.920
And in that point, cloud and
teleradiology is inseparable.

00:16:38.920 --> 00:16:40.870
And the cloud is essential.

00:16:40.870 --> 00:16:41.370
Thank you.

00:16:44.980 --> 00:16:47.320
DANIEL MOLLURA: Cloud
brings down costs

00:16:47.320 --> 00:16:49.570
and lowers complexity.

00:16:49.570 --> 00:16:52.780
It's the basic two
deliverables that a cloud

00:16:52.780 --> 00:16:56.120
can bring into a
low-resource environment.

00:16:56.120 --> 00:17:00.160
So for example, this past year,
Rad-Aid with Google Cloud,

00:17:00.160 --> 00:17:04.180
as well as Ambra and a
logistics company, Tribalco,

00:17:04.180 --> 00:17:07.450
implemented a solution
where there was Lao Friends

00:17:07.450 --> 00:17:08.490
Hospital for Children.

00:17:08.490 --> 00:17:11.020
This was a low-resource
hospital in Laos.

00:17:11.020 --> 00:17:13.540
Had no radiology department
at all, practically,

00:17:13.540 --> 00:17:15.849
three or four years ago
when we started there.

00:17:15.849 --> 00:17:18.730
Now for them to have
PACS, right, this

00:17:18.730 --> 00:17:22.390
is the architecture for sending
the images from the scanners

00:17:22.390 --> 00:17:23.530
to the workstations.

00:17:23.530 --> 00:17:26.543
For them to have done that
using a conventional solution,

00:17:26.543 --> 00:17:27.460
Rad-Aid has done that.

00:17:27.460 --> 00:17:31.420
We've sent these large racks,
these large servers to places

00:17:31.420 --> 00:17:36.190
that had to build a
climate-controlled server room

00:17:36.190 --> 00:17:37.840
and do their own storage.

00:17:37.840 --> 00:17:40.270
But that was very
complex and very costly.

00:17:40.270 --> 00:17:42.040
Imagine a low-resource
hospital having

00:17:42.040 --> 00:17:44.020
to build all that
infrastructure and then

00:17:44.020 --> 00:17:46.570
have all that IT personnel
within the hospital

00:17:46.570 --> 00:17:48.160
to manage it.

00:17:48.160 --> 00:17:51.460
It's a very difficult
scenario to manage.

00:17:51.460 --> 00:17:55.010
So instead, so the pilot we
did with Google Cloud in Laos,

00:17:55.010 --> 00:17:58.690
for example, we came down
with a compact server

00:17:58.690 --> 00:18:02.080
that we can hand carry
that's temperature-resistant

00:18:02.080 --> 00:18:05.920
so it didn't need any
climate control at all

00:18:05.920 --> 00:18:10.570
and had all the preloaded Ambra
PACS software onboard already.

00:18:10.570 --> 00:18:12.910
Went to the hospital
and turned it on,

00:18:12.910 --> 00:18:15.130
connected it to the
imaging modalities

00:18:15.130 --> 00:18:17.110
so that it would
have onsite storage,

00:18:17.110 --> 00:18:18.970
but as well it would
immediately backup

00:18:18.970 --> 00:18:21.850
to the cloud, which was
the Google Cloud, which

00:18:21.850 --> 00:18:25.760
we're calling Rad-Aid Friendship
Cloud in this instance.

00:18:25.760 --> 00:18:29.890
So it overcame all of these
logistical barriers that

00:18:29.890 --> 00:18:31.550
could have occurred otherwise.

00:18:31.550 --> 00:18:34.220
And then it also dramatically
lowered the cost.

00:18:34.220 --> 00:18:37.120
So what cloud introduces
is it lowers the barrier

00:18:37.120 --> 00:18:39.460
to entry for
low-resource hospitals

00:18:39.460 --> 00:18:42.310
to implement their IT
infrastructure by having

00:18:42.310 --> 00:18:46.000
a way to really turnkey
transfer images from scanners

00:18:46.000 --> 00:18:49.260
to workstations and then have
a way to back up those images.

00:18:50.680 --> 00:18:52.180
RAFAEL DE CASTRO
FIGUEROA: I'm going

00:18:52.180 --> 00:18:56.525
to share one technical
example that we have.

00:18:56.525 --> 00:18:58.900
For machine learning, we used
TensorFlow for the last two

00:18:58.900 --> 00:19:00.640
years, since the first versions.

00:19:00.640 --> 00:19:04.150
And back then we had, I
think, about 10 models,

00:19:04.150 --> 00:19:05.830
because these models
are very narrow.

00:19:05.830 --> 00:19:08.050
So it's one model
for a chest X-ray.

00:19:08.050 --> 00:19:10.950
We're not going to work in AKGs,
we're not going to work in TCs.

00:19:10.950 --> 00:19:12.220
They're very specific.

00:19:12.220 --> 00:19:15.490
And every time I tried to
train one of those models

00:19:15.490 --> 00:19:19.360
in our own VMs, for example,
we took about five weeks

00:19:19.360 --> 00:19:22.610
for each of these models to get
train in [INAUDIBLE] examples.

00:19:22.610 --> 00:19:25.630
So just imagine the R&amp;D
cycle for the developers who

00:19:25.630 --> 00:19:28.800
have to wait five weeks, see
if the model is better or not

00:19:28.800 --> 00:19:30.490
than the last version.

00:19:30.490 --> 00:19:32.440
So we've been through
a program that Google

00:19:32.440 --> 00:19:33.920
has called Launchpad Studio--

00:19:33.920 --> 00:19:36.580
it's an acceleration
program for tech companies--

00:19:36.580 --> 00:19:37.330
last year.

00:19:37.330 --> 00:19:40.180
And our goal was to apply
the continuous integration

00:19:40.180 --> 00:19:42.380
[? parroting ?] in
machine learning.

00:19:42.380 --> 00:19:45.670
So the thought of continuous
delivering new models.

00:19:45.670 --> 00:19:49.450
And the way that we did that,
we port all the models that we

00:19:49.450 --> 00:19:55.420
have for a Cloud ML Engine,
which is managed TensorFlow,

00:19:55.420 --> 00:19:57.910
that you can just choose
the metric and the data

00:19:57.910 --> 00:20:00.740
set and it trains it for you.

00:20:00.740 --> 00:20:05.210
So what used to take five weeks
right now is taking two days.

00:20:05.210 --> 00:20:07.850
So how it works now,
we get data sets,

00:20:07.850 --> 00:20:10.930
for example, for chest X-ray,
is one of the simplest one.

00:20:10.930 --> 00:20:13.180
We have a list of findings--

00:20:13.180 --> 00:20:16.360
nodules, TB, pneumonia,
[? consolidation. ?]

00:20:16.360 --> 00:20:19.450
And each of those models,
instead of just one

00:20:19.450 --> 00:20:21.790
being trained in a
VM for five weeks,

00:20:21.790 --> 00:20:23.710
we can put to 100
different containers

00:20:23.710 --> 00:20:26.800
to be trained at same time,
and each of those containers

00:20:26.800 --> 00:20:30.370
have different neural network
architectures, different layer

00:20:30.370 --> 00:20:34.000
sizes, and different
pre-processing techniques

00:20:34.000 --> 00:20:34.780
on this.

00:20:34.780 --> 00:20:37.960
And because it's pretty much
a trial and error still, deep

00:20:37.960 --> 00:20:40.660
learning, you don't
know exactly what

00:20:40.660 --> 00:20:42.950
will impact if you change
layers, for example.

00:20:42.950 --> 00:20:44.990
So what we do is put
these 100 containers

00:20:44.990 --> 00:20:47.670
to be trained at the same
time with just one script

00:20:47.670 --> 00:20:48.970
to call this API--

00:20:48.970 --> 00:20:51.520
each of these containers will
have a different configuration

00:20:51.520 --> 00:20:53.080
of the neural net, for example.

00:20:53.080 --> 00:20:56.710
And then after one to two
days, the 100 are trained

00:20:56.710 --> 00:20:58.030
and we collect the metrics.

00:20:58.030 --> 00:21:00.180
But instead of collecting
the regular metrics

00:21:00.180 --> 00:21:02.500
of memory, latency,
[INAUDIBLE] for example,

00:21:02.500 --> 00:21:03.830
we collect those as well.

00:21:03.830 --> 00:21:06.497
But we also collect the metrics
from the confusion matrix

00:21:06.497 --> 00:21:07.450
in TensorFlow.

00:21:07.450 --> 00:21:12.100
So accuracy and recall, these
are collected automatically.

00:21:12.100 --> 00:21:15.520
And the model that has the best
performance go to production,

00:21:15.520 --> 00:21:18.100
the other 99 are killed,
and this is started again.

00:21:18.100 --> 00:21:21.100
So each 48 hours we retrain
our whole models again

00:21:21.100 --> 00:21:24.160
with the new data that is
coming from these 500 hospitals.

00:21:24.160 --> 00:21:26.650
And right now this is
happening without us

00:21:26.650 --> 00:21:28.150
having to press any buttons.

00:21:28.150 --> 00:21:30.760
It's happening-- actually,
we're doing this weekly.

00:21:30.760 --> 00:21:33.670
So every Saturday night
we train with the new data

00:21:33.670 --> 00:21:37.840
from the week, but with these
variations of architectures

00:21:37.840 --> 00:21:39.500
and layers and stuff like this.

00:21:39.500 --> 00:21:41.500
So right now-- and
since we've got this

00:21:41.500 --> 00:21:43.810
about eight months
ago, right now

00:21:43.810 --> 00:21:46.750
we have 200 different
models running in production

00:21:46.750 --> 00:21:48.530
classifying these diseases.

00:21:48.530 --> 00:21:52.300
So this is one example of
how it became scalable,

00:21:52.300 --> 00:21:57.730
and now our ML engineers can
focus on the models itself

00:21:57.730 --> 00:21:59.560
and not in the plumbing around.

00:21:59.560 --> 00:22:02.890
And when you stop to see
machine learning work,

00:22:02.890 --> 00:22:06.110
I would say that about 90%
of the work is the plumbing.

00:22:06.110 --> 00:22:07.650
It's the authentication.

00:22:07.650 --> 00:22:09.730
It's data engineering,
organizing, ingesting

00:22:09.730 --> 00:22:10.390
this data.

00:22:10.390 --> 00:22:14.020
And 10% is actually PhD
machine learning work.

00:22:14.020 --> 00:22:18.410
And if you give these PhDs
to do the other parts,

00:22:18.410 --> 00:22:21.110
do the DevOps part, they
would take like three,

00:22:21.110 --> 00:22:24.610
four times what a good
cloud developer would take.

00:22:24.610 --> 00:22:30.850
So it's a way to speed up the ML
work, which is actually usually

00:22:30.850 --> 00:22:34.990
high paying per hour people that
don't know how to do the cloud

00:22:34.990 --> 00:22:38.020
infrastructure, but they're
very good at math, mathematics.

00:22:38.020 --> 00:22:41.642
So to keep them focused
on what they're good at

00:22:41.642 --> 00:22:43.600
and the cloud take care
of the plumbing around.

00:22:46.630 --> 00:22:50.040
ARIE MEIR: So I guess when
you bring up a AI and medicine

00:22:50.040 --> 00:22:51.970
or radiology, the
natural questions

00:22:51.970 --> 00:22:56.430
is what is the role
that AI will play

00:22:56.430 --> 00:22:59.957
in changing the dynamics
between a doctor and a patient?

00:22:59.957 --> 00:23:02.290
And there is the elephant in
the room, kind of question,

00:23:02.290 --> 00:23:05.910
will AI replace doctors,
maybe, in certain specialties?

00:23:05.910 --> 00:23:08.640
How do you use AI
in your organization

00:23:08.640 --> 00:23:11.820
and what's your take on these
changing roles of a doctor,

00:23:11.820 --> 00:23:14.640
and will AI replace
or augment doctors?

00:23:17.160 --> 00:23:23.750
LORENZO RIZZATTO: Well, in
our opinion, in my opinion, AI

00:23:23.750 --> 00:23:25.620
augments doctors.

00:23:25.620 --> 00:23:35.670
And so far in our organization
we implemented some models

00:23:35.670 --> 00:23:39.630
for prioritizing, as
you mentioned before,

00:23:39.630 --> 00:23:45.030
and also to, let's
say, to segment

00:23:45.030 --> 00:23:50.010
the studies in order
to assign the studies

00:23:50.010 --> 00:23:51.150
to the right doctor.

00:23:51.150 --> 00:23:54.930
So, I don't know, for
example, mammography.

00:23:54.930 --> 00:24:03.900
Dense breast, so more
difficult, of course, to report.

00:24:03.900 --> 00:24:10.710
So what the model does is
a classification of breast

00:24:10.710 --> 00:24:18.090
density, and then if the
study is a dense breast,

00:24:18.090 --> 00:24:23.050
it goes to an
expert radiologist.

00:24:23.050 --> 00:24:29.130
And so this lowers the
risk for the patient.

00:24:29.130 --> 00:24:34.460
In my opinion, in the future
it will be more and more.

00:24:34.460 --> 00:24:40.890
So why not, maybe,
radiologist will be replaced,

00:24:40.890 --> 00:24:45.150
maybe for routine
works, routine reporting

00:24:45.150 --> 00:24:49.890
and we'll have more time to
concentrate on, let's say,

00:24:49.890 --> 00:24:51.870
most difficult cases.

00:24:51.870 --> 00:24:52.440
I believe so.

00:24:55.763 --> 00:24:57.180
TAKASHI TOCHI:
Talking about AI, I

00:24:57.180 --> 00:25:00.180
want to share the
ironical situation

00:25:00.180 --> 00:25:05.300
in Japan, ironical situation
of approval in Japan.

00:25:05.300 --> 00:25:09.210
Through the collaboration
with start up AI vendors,

00:25:09.210 --> 00:25:12.710
we have developed several
AIs, and our doctors,

00:25:12.710 --> 00:25:14.950
and all the doctors
have used them

00:25:14.950 --> 00:25:18.600
since more than two years ago.

00:25:18.600 --> 00:25:24.560
So now they know a lot about
AI, good and bad, its usefulness

00:25:24.560 --> 00:25:27.380
and its limits both.

00:25:27.380 --> 00:25:31.970
But maybe we Japanese are
very conservative people.

00:25:31.970 --> 00:25:37.370
And our national health ministry
has approved just one AI

00:25:37.370 --> 00:25:38.840
by now.

00:25:38.840 --> 00:25:41.690
Many vendors have
developed many AIs.

00:25:41.690 --> 00:25:44.900
But still just one approval.

00:25:44.900 --> 00:25:47.600
And on the media
or among doctors,

00:25:47.600 --> 00:25:53.330
the pros and cons of AI still
remains as a matter of dispute,

00:25:53.330 --> 00:25:58.460
but from the viewpoint of our
doctors, the doctors with lots

00:25:58.460 --> 00:26:02.180
of experience of AI,
from their point of view

00:26:02.180 --> 00:26:06.290
that dispute seems to be
irrational, one-dimensional.

00:26:06.290 --> 00:26:09.250
For example, some
doctors expect too much.

00:26:09.250 --> 00:26:14.450
Oh, AI's going to do many,
many things with 100% accuracy.

00:26:14.450 --> 00:26:15.470
No, no, no.

00:26:15.470 --> 00:26:19.010
Or some others fear too much.

00:26:19.010 --> 00:26:21.750
Oh, AI's going to drive
us out of business.

00:26:21.750 --> 00:26:22.860
No.

00:26:22.860 --> 00:26:26.420
My take is, just give
them the opportunity

00:26:26.420 --> 00:26:30.380
of hands-on AI trial
as soon as possible.

00:26:30.380 --> 00:26:34.790
After a couple of weeks of
using AI, maybe they know,

00:26:34.790 --> 00:26:38.570
they will realize that
reality, good and bad.

00:26:38.570 --> 00:26:43.790
And that dispute would be more
realistic, more down to earth.

00:26:43.790 --> 00:26:48.020
And I think that would be
beneficial for our health

00:26:48.020 --> 00:26:48.860
ministry too.

00:26:52.042 --> 00:26:53.750
DANIEL MOLLURA: This
is a great question.

00:26:53.750 --> 00:26:56.420
I'm a radiologist, so I'm
right in the middle of this.

00:26:56.420 --> 00:26:59.620
Is AI going to replace
me, for example?

00:26:59.620 --> 00:27:02.830
Most of us believe
at this point that AI

00:27:02.830 --> 00:27:06.880
is going to increase quality
and increase efficiency,

00:27:06.880 --> 00:27:11.170
but it can't really replace
the human caregiver.

00:27:11.170 --> 00:27:14.050
Human patients really
need a human caregiver.

00:27:14.050 --> 00:27:16.300
It's the human condition.

00:27:16.300 --> 00:27:19.840
So no one's trying to advocate
that machines should take over

00:27:19.840 --> 00:27:21.430
this particular role.

00:27:21.430 --> 00:27:24.490
But it can improve
the speed at which

00:27:24.490 --> 00:27:27.380
we do things and the quality
at which we do things.

00:27:27.380 --> 00:27:29.890
So a couple of
examples have been

00:27:29.890 --> 00:27:34.000
suggested, like a
basic triage role,

00:27:34.000 --> 00:27:36.070
for triaging the
different cases--

00:27:36.070 --> 00:27:37.930
ones that are
abnormal from normal

00:27:37.930 --> 00:27:40.510
or ones that deserve immediate
attention versus ones

00:27:40.510 --> 00:27:41.590
that can wait--

00:27:41.590 --> 00:27:44.500
acute disease or
emergency versus something

00:27:44.500 --> 00:27:48.090
that's chronic and can be dealt
with at a different velocity

00:27:48.090 --> 00:27:49.680
in the care context.

00:27:49.680 --> 00:27:54.590
So these are different
scenarios that AI can deliver.

00:27:54.590 --> 00:27:57.040
But what's very true, and
what was just mentioned,

00:27:57.040 --> 00:28:00.400
is that AI is really
not in our context yet.

00:28:00.400 --> 00:28:02.380
When I go to work
on a daily basis

00:28:02.380 --> 00:28:07.370
there is no AI that I'm
aware of in my midst.

00:28:07.370 --> 00:28:08.570
But it is in the future.

00:28:08.570 --> 00:28:10.270
So we're trying
to incorporate it

00:28:10.270 --> 00:28:14.410
into the vision of what
the profession will have.

00:28:14.410 --> 00:28:16.220
But what's also
important, though,

00:28:16.220 --> 00:28:18.610
is that once you
start integrating AI

00:28:18.610 --> 00:28:20.920
into the clinical
care workflow, you're

00:28:20.920 --> 00:28:23.590
really changing
the way that doctor

00:28:23.590 --> 00:28:25.960
is going to do their job.

00:28:25.960 --> 00:28:28.260
And that means
retraining that person

00:28:28.260 --> 00:28:31.020
for how their job
is now influenced

00:28:31.020 --> 00:28:33.600
by this new capability.

00:28:33.600 --> 00:28:35.765
So it doesn't
transfer very easily.

00:28:35.765 --> 00:28:37.140
It doesn't just
happen overnight,

00:28:37.140 --> 00:28:39.750
because that doctor is still
responsible for the life

00:28:39.750 --> 00:28:40.940
of the patient.

00:28:40.940 --> 00:28:42.690
So they're not just
going to hand it over.

00:28:42.690 --> 00:28:46.500
So like you just heard, only
one approval of an AI mechanism

00:28:46.500 --> 00:28:47.040
so far.

00:28:47.040 --> 00:28:49.260
I mean, that's not
surprising because we're

00:28:49.260 --> 00:28:53.370
talking about the fact that the
human care on the other side

00:28:53.370 --> 00:28:56.110
is still at risk if
that AI doesn't work.

00:28:56.110 --> 00:28:59.430
So that transfer needs
to happen in a way that's

00:28:59.430 --> 00:29:00.510
careful and trained.

00:29:00.510 --> 00:29:02.790
So I think what
comes from this is

00:29:02.790 --> 00:29:06.900
a great interest in education
for health providers

00:29:06.900 --> 00:29:08.400
to really think
about how they would

00:29:08.400 --> 00:29:12.500
use AI in those workflows.

00:29:12.500 --> 00:29:14.000
RAFAEL DE CASTRO
FIGUEROA: I'm going

00:29:14.000 --> 00:29:18.030
to open with a fun
quote, that, what is AI?

00:29:18.030 --> 00:29:23.248
Because I heard, like, if it's
reading in Python and C++,

00:29:23.248 --> 00:29:24.540
it's probably machine learning.

00:29:24.540 --> 00:29:27.490
If it's reading in
PowerPoint, it's probably AI.

00:29:27.490 --> 00:29:32.370
And so we need to differentiate
things and in our case,

00:29:32.370 --> 00:29:35.580
was a matter of
adoption for the doctors

00:29:35.580 --> 00:29:37.710
because we started to
trust these models with how

00:29:37.710 --> 00:29:39.570
we approach doctors who use it.

00:29:39.570 --> 00:29:41.610
And in our case, in
health care specifically,

00:29:41.610 --> 00:29:44.090
cannot be an end in itself.

00:29:44.090 --> 00:29:46.210
It need to be a
mean to something.

00:29:46.210 --> 00:29:48.900
And once we started to do
a lot of data diagnostics

00:29:48.900 --> 00:29:51.030
we started to hit the
first bottleneck, which

00:29:51.030 --> 00:29:52.560
was prioritization.

00:29:52.560 --> 00:29:54.960
So even if we give the
results in same day,

00:29:54.960 --> 00:29:58.110
24 hours can be too much,
depending on the case.

00:29:58.110 --> 00:30:00.840
So we approached doctors
in a very light way.

00:30:00.840 --> 00:30:04.200
What do you think if you
prioritized emergencies?

00:30:04.200 --> 00:30:07.260
And then we ran about six
months of a dual pipeline

00:30:07.260 --> 00:30:10.360
of the predictions
against a regular nurse

00:30:10.360 --> 00:30:11.632
prioritizing things for them.

00:30:11.632 --> 00:30:13.590
We have a nurse full time
to take care of this.

00:30:13.590 --> 00:30:16.140
And It was obvious
that the prioritization

00:30:16.140 --> 00:30:19.290
made by ML models was
being better and faster--

00:30:19.290 --> 00:30:20.310
milliseconds.

00:30:20.310 --> 00:30:21.840
And so they agreed.

00:30:21.840 --> 00:30:23.910
And then after one
year using this,

00:30:23.910 --> 00:30:27.660
when do we hit about 90%
recall, which is very low false

00:30:27.660 --> 00:30:31.500
negatives-- we train everything
for recall first, specificity--

00:30:31.500 --> 00:30:34.170
we say, OK, now we trust
enough in these models

00:30:34.170 --> 00:30:35.800
to do a double check.

00:30:35.800 --> 00:30:39.300
So if the algorithm predicts,
for example, pneumothorax,

00:30:39.300 --> 00:30:41.780
that could be fatal
if not treated fast--

00:30:41.780 --> 00:30:43.650
you move it up in
the queue in seconds

00:30:43.650 --> 00:30:44.940
to get to the radiology.

00:30:44.940 --> 00:30:46.560
But if it clicks
normal, he didn't

00:30:46.560 --> 00:30:49.290
see it because you can have
very small pneumothorax,

00:30:49.290 --> 00:30:51.930
then the system sends
to three other doctors

00:30:51.930 --> 00:30:53.865
and create a board of
consensus between them.

00:30:53.865 --> 00:30:55.740
Of course, it's a blind
test for all of them.

00:30:55.740 --> 00:30:57.120
They think they're
first opinion.

00:30:57.120 --> 00:30:58.662
But then with this,
we could rule out

00:30:58.662 --> 00:31:00.330
these kind of mistakes.

00:31:00.330 --> 00:31:01.610
And then a data point--

00:31:01.610 --> 00:31:02.610
it's not me saying this.

00:31:02.610 --> 00:31:05.130
It's a study by Harvard,
which is the doctor

00:31:05.130 --> 00:31:07.800
precision is around 80%.

00:31:07.800 --> 00:31:10.050
Depend of the modality,
of course, but when

00:31:10.050 --> 00:31:13.680
you stop and think about
it, of 100 stage one

00:31:13.680 --> 00:31:18.420
cancer with a very small nodule,
80 will be found and 20 not.

00:31:18.420 --> 00:31:23.340
Probably going to be diagnosed
late with a stage two or three.

00:31:23.340 --> 00:31:25.110
So now when you
approach a doctor,

00:31:25.110 --> 00:31:27.570
OK, if you made a
mistake, the system

00:31:27.570 --> 00:31:31.390
will send to three other doctor,
so this kind of small things

00:31:31.390 --> 00:31:33.930
are not going to be
a liability for you.

00:31:33.930 --> 00:31:35.430
And then they said
OK, let's try it.

00:31:35.430 --> 00:31:35.930
Same thing.

00:31:35.930 --> 00:31:39.760
We did a dual pipeline and they
started to trust the models.

00:31:39.760 --> 00:31:41.430
And now we are using this--

00:31:41.430 --> 00:31:43.820
of those 200 models that
we have, with all of them

00:31:43.820 --> 00:31:45.660
for prioritization,
and we have 40

00:31:45.660 --> 00:31:47.760
that we trust enough
to do the double check.

00:31:47.760 --> 00:31:50.230
And so it's a matter
of how you approach it.

00:31:50.230 --> 00:31:55.950
And last example is that what
is the KPIs that the doctors are

00:31:55.950 --> 00:31:56.490
looking for?

00:31:56.490 --> 00:31:59.840
Is different KPIs for us as the
company managing this platform,

00:31:59.840 --> 00:32:01.650
but for them, most
of the time it's

00:32:01.650 --> 00:32:05.520
how many diagnostics I can
do per hour in a safe way.

00:32:05.520 --> 00:32:07.780
Because the [? unit ?]
economics need to make sense.

00:32:07.780 --> 00:32:10.890
To have good doctors on
board doing these diagnostics

00:32:10.890 --> 00:32:14.220
they need to get they
same dollar per hour

00:32:14.220 --> 00:32:17.700
as they will have in a hospital,
in a shift in the hospital,

00:32:17.700 --> 00:32:18.640
for example.

00:32:18.640 --> 00:32:22.530
So the way to speed up this
things with this prioritization

00:32:22.530 --> 00:32:24.480
and double check, and
with decision trees

00:32:24.480 --> 00:32:26.058
that they click instead of type.

00:32:26.058 --> 00:32:27.600
Like, for example,
a neurologist will

00:32:27.600 --> 00:32:31.000
click epilepsy, diffuse,
frontal lobe, diagnose.

00:32:31.000 --> 00:32:34.770
So with this, we speed up about
10 times how many diagnostics

00:32:34.770 --> 00:32:35.770
they do per hour.

00:32:35.770 --> 00:32:38.970
But they know if they misclick
something-- it will happen,

00:32:38.970 --> 00:32:40.860
they do thousands per day--

00:32:40.860 --> 00:32:42.270
this will go to other doctors.

00:32:42.270 --> 00:32:45.390
So it was all a matter
of how you approach them

00:32:45.390 --> 00:32:48.135
and how you show the benefit,
and not the technology

00:32:48.135 --> 00:32:51.000
itself they actually don't care
if it's running in TensorFlow

00:32:51.000 --> 00:32:52.620
in the cloud and on
premise, but they

00:32:52.620 --> 00:32:56.070
need to know that is helping
them to make it safe.

00:32:56.070 --> 00:32:59.920
So we believe that when you
combine the doctor and the ML

00:32:59.920 --> 00:33:04.870
models you can hit 99% accuracy.

00:33:04.870 --> 00:33:06.520
ARIE MEIR: Lots of good ideas.

00:33:06.520 --> 00:33:10.340
One thing I would like to
share from our perspective,

00:33:10.340 --> 00:33:13.090
AI is not going to
replace radiology,

00:33:13.090 --> 00:33:17.730
but I think radiologists
that use AI could replace

00:33:17.730 --> 00:33:19.710
radiologists that don't use AI.

00:33:19.710 --> 00:33:21.270
Because the AI is
yet another tool.

00:33:21.270 --> 00:33:22.890
It's like saying I'm not
going to use a computer.

00:33:22.890 --> 00:33:24.200
I'm not going to use
digital technology.

00:33:24.200 --> 00:33:25.740
I'm not going to
use the internet.

00:33:25.740 --> 00:33:29.653
It's just going to make the
quality and the productivity

00:33:29.653 --> 00:33:31.180
at lower level.

00:33:31.180 --> 00:33:35.010
So we think AI might be another
tool in the arsenal of doctors

00:33:35.010 --> 00:33:36.630
that are helping patients.

00:33:36.630 --> 00:33:38.970
In this context I
want to-- this topic

00:33:38.970 --> 00:33:40.920
came up, like
numbers and economics

00:33:40.920 --> 00:33:41.850
and who pays for this.

00:33:41.850 --> 00:33:43.770
So I'm actually
curious, so Dan, you

00:33:43.770 --> 00:33:46.470
represent the non-profit
sector and you folks

00:33:46.470 --> 00:33:48.420
represent the commercial
organizations.

00:33:48.420 --> 00:33:50.970
So in your geographies
and your markets,

00:33:50.970 --> 00:33:56.550
what kind of economic trends or,
maybe, forces you are seeing?

00:33:56.550 --> 00:33:59.400
Like for example
in the US, where

00:33:59.400 --> 00:34:01.620
there is a payer and the
provider and oftentimes

00:34:01.620 --> 00:34:03.780
they have this interesting
balance between them,

00:34:03.780 --> 00:34:08.010
who's going to pay for this
new AI service portfolio?

00:34:08.010 --> 00:34:10.960
How does it play
in your markets?

00:34:10.960 --> 00:34:14.580
LORENZO RIZZATTO: This is a
very interesting question.

00:34:14.580 --> 00:34:22.420
Well, to tell you the truth,
for example, in Italy,

00:34:22.420 --> 00:34:29.060
the payer is usually
the government.

00:34:29.060 --> 00:34:32.040
So we have a public
health system.

00:34:32.040 --> 00:34:39.139
And they have really,
a lot of difficulties

00:34:39.139 --> 00:34:44.090
in incorporating
these new technologies

00:34:44.090 --> 00:34:46.010
into their payments.

00:34:49.710 --> 00:34:53.409
So far, for example,
not even teleradiology

00:34:53.409 --> 00:34:58.330
is inside these
lists of services

00:34:58.330 --> 00:35:00.820
that the government pays.

00:35:00.820 --> 00:35:12.880
So of course, since artificial
intelligence algorithms

00:35:12.880 --> 00:35:19.100
lower the risks, and,
at the end, of course,

00:35:19.100 --> 00:35:24.310
they make you save money.

00:35:24.310 --> 00:35:30.130
In my opinion, for example,
the radiologists themselves

00:35:30.130 --> 00:35:36.010
and the organizations and,
for example, the insurances

00:35:36.010 --> 00:35:42.460
that cover the organizations
and the radiologists

00:35:42.460 --> 00:35:47.365
should pay for these tools
because the risk lowers.

00:35:49.970 --> 00:35:53.180
TAKASHI TOCHI: I can't tell
the financial aspect of AI,

00:35:53.180 --> 00:35:57.890
but as I mentioned, in
Japan the number of scanners

00:35:57.890 --> 00:35:59.260
are very, very many.

00:35:59.260 --> 00:36:02.960
We have many scanners but
the number of radiologists

00:36:02.960 --> 00:36:05.360
is limited.

00:36:05.360 --> 00:36:09.710
So to cover that shortfall we
need not only teleradiology

00:36:09.710 --> 00:36:13.790
but we need also AI.

00:36:13.790 --> 00:36:16.930
I can't tell to what
extent, but more or less, AI

00:36:16.930 --> 00:36:20.550
will be introduced in medicine.

00:36:20.550 --> 00:36:24.170
And AI will work together
with radiologists

00:36:24.170 --> 00:36:26.750
and then cover that shortfall.

00:36:26.750 --> 00:36:33.040
In that context, Japan ranks
top as a country that needs

00:36:33.040 --> 00:36:35.090
AI and teleradiology both.

00:36:38.167 --> 00:36:39.750
DANIEL MOLLURA: To
answer the question

00:36:39.750 --> 00:36:43.770
about who would pay for AI,
first I would put it out there

00:36:43.770 --> 00:36:47.520
for all of you to think
about, how exactly will AI

00:36:47.520 --> 00:36:52.560
be delivered to these clients,
these medical clients?

00:36:52.560 --> 00:36:54.900
Because right now,
if you think about AI

00:36:54.900 --> 00:36:58.740
coming about within
PACS, well, then that

00:36:58.740 --> 00:37:03.810
means that a PACS vendor
will license or build itself

00:37:03.810 --> 00:37:05.650
AI and put it into the product.

00:37:05.650 --> 00:37:08.190
Remember, PACS is
a closed system.

00:37:08.190 --> 00:37:11.010
No one can just open up PACS
and add new software into it.

00:37:11.010 --> 00:37:11.570
It's closed.

00:37:11.570 --> 00:37:15.353
So it goes to the client and
then they just use it as is.

00:37:15.353 --> 00:37:16.770
So essentially
that hospital would

00:37:16.770 --> 00:37:19.020
be paying the PACS vendor
for whatever is in it.

00:37:19.020 --> 00:37:22.950
So they would be passing that
cost through to the hospital

00:37:22.950 --> 00:37:24.510
through the price of the PACS.

00:37:24.510 --> 00:37:25.680
Same goes for hardware.

00:37:25.680 --> 00:37:28.710
If you think about
CT, ultrasound, MRI,

00:37:28.710 --> 00:37:31.260
or mammography
machinery, that can also

00:37:31.260 --> 00:37:33.960
embed AI within the equipment.

00:37:33.960 --> 00:37:37.410
So again, it'll be included
as product advancements that

00:37:37.410 --> 00:37:41.790
then get sold to the hospitals
in that kind of dynamic.

00:37:41.790 --> 00:37:45.030
Then when we think about, well,
who pays for it short term,

00:37:45.030 --> 00:37:46.710
middle term, long
term, that's where

00:37:46.710 --> 00:37:49.470
the difference between
nonprofit and private sector

00:37:49.470 --> 00:37:52.420
and public sector actually
becomes very relevant,

00:37:52.420 --> 00:37:55.200
especially in low and
middle-income countries.

00:37:55.200 --> 00:37:59.400
As a nonprofit, we have a
much higher risk tolerance

00:37:59.400 --> 00:38:02.490
than any of the others do in
private sector and government

00:38:02.490 --> 00:38:06.690
because we're mission-driven
and we can afford to lose money.

00:38:06.690 --> 00:38:08.880
We can go and do something
for the sake of mission

00:38:08.880 --> 00:38:10.590
to put something
in place because we

00:38:10.590 --> 00:38:12.000
know the patients need it.

00:38:12.000 --> 00:38:15.660
So in the short term, a
nonprofit like Rad-Aid

00:38:15.660 --> 00:38:18.720
can have a very high
tolerance for that risk,

00:38:18.720 --> 00:38:21.450
whereas private sector will
be very reluctant to take risk

00:38:21.450 --> 00:38:22.890
like that initially.

00:38:22.890 --> 00:38:26.130
But then once nonprofit can
show that something is workable,

00:38:26.130 --> 00:38:29.250
a pilot or something
is sustainable,

00:38:29.250 --> 00:38:32.430
that that's where that
risk kind of reduces

00:38:32.430 --> 00:38:34.350
and that return on
investment becomes more

00:38:34.350 --> 00:38:36.113
palatable to private sector.

00:38:36.113 --> 00:38:37.530
And that's why
it's very important

00:38:37.530 --> 00:38:42.870
to consider the private and
nonprofit sector partnerships.

00:38:42.870 --> 00:38:44.580
Then as you get into
the middle term,

00:38:44.580 --> 00:38:48.960
that medium term of nonprofits
and private sectors partnering

00:38:48.960 --> 00:38:51.510
in that regard, that's
when government really does

00:38:51.510 --> 00:38:54.090
come into play as a
long-term solution.

00:38:54.090 --> 00:38:55.825
Because then you're
bringing in the fact

00:38:55.825 --> 00:38:57.450
that governments are
never going to get

00:38:57.450 --> 00:39:01.125
involved in that initial stuff
that actually has high risk.

00:39:01.125 --> 00:39:02.740
That nonprofits can do.

00:39:02.740 --> 00:39:05.550
But for long term,
for public sector

00:39:05.550 --> 00:39:09.330
and providing public
goods to the population,

00:39:09.330 --> 00:39:10.990
that makes more
sense for government.

00:39:10.990 --> 00:39:14.580
So short term, for
high risk innovation,

00:39:14.580 --> 00:39:17.190
nonprofits are
well-positioned in this space.

00:39:17.190 --> 00:39:18.690
And then for short
to middle term,

00:39:18.690 --> 00:39:20.820
you've got private
sector partnerships.

00:39:20.820 --> 00:39:23.070
And then for the long term
you have a government actor

00:39:23.070 --> 00:39:24.120
also coming together.

00:39:24.120 --> 00:39:25.740
And that's how a
lot of innovations

00:39:25.740 --> 00:39:27.593
have come into
health care, and I

00:39:27.593 --> 00:39:29.010
would say that
it's probably going

00:39:29.010 --> 00:39:32.583
to be along similar
lines in this case, too.

00:39:32.583 --> 00:39:34.000
RAFAEL DE CASTRO
FIGUEROA: I agree

00:39:34.000 --> 00:39:37.120
with what Dan said about
how AI will be delivered.

00:39:37.120 --> 00:39:40.150
I think this is the main point,
to think who will pay for it.

00:39:40.150 --> 00:39:44.230
And this is, in our
case of health care,

00:39:44.230 --> 00:39:46.150
linked to regulation again.

00:39:46.150 --> 00:39:49.240
So for example, here in the
US you can do teleconsulting.

00:39:49.240 --> 00:39:51.490
So there is doctor on
demand, teledoctor.

00:39:51.490 --> 00:39:54.310
You talk to your
doctor through a video.

00:39:54.310 --> 00:39:57.100
In Brazil you cannot do this,
you need to be B2B, not B2C.

00:39:57.100 --> 00:39:58.890
Never a patient can
call us directly.

00:39:58.890 --> 00:40:01.113
You need to go to a
local small clinic.

00:40:01.113 --> 00:40:03.280
And this clinic will then
access all the specialists

00:40:03.280 --> 00:40:04.660
in the hospitals.

00:40:04.660 --> 00:40:07.240
If you follow these rules,
you are doing a B2B--

00:40:07.240 --> 00:40:10.360
it can be a video call,
but our main goal is exams,

00:40:10.360 --> 00:40:13.000
diagnostics on top of
X-rays, MRIs, et cetera--

00:40:13.000 --> 00:40:14.780
then it's fully reimbursed.

00:40:14.780 --> 00:40:16.780
So it doesn't matter
if it's a local doctor

00:40:16.780 --> 00:40:19.190
or is a remote
doctor in Sao Paulo.

00:40:19.190 --> 00:40:23.340
The government or the insurers
will reimburse a teleconsulting

00:40:23.340 --> 00:40:25.120
or telediagnostics.

00:40:25.120 --> 00:40:28.120
So the way that we did,
we embedded our AI costs,

00:40:28.120 --> 00:40:31.240
our machine learning costs
per test, per report.

00:40:31.240 --> 00:40:34.640
So whenever report change hands,
we receive an amount for this

00:40:34.640 --> 00:40:35.870
and the doctor get paid.

00:40:35.870 --> 00:40:37.775
And the engineering
cost of GPU, these

00:40:37.775 --> 00:40:39.890
are like GPU hours or API calls.

00:40:39.890 --> 00:40:42.650
But there is a call,
there is a price per test.

00:40:42.650 --> 00:40:45.250
So when you embed
this per report

00:40:45.250 --> 00:40:47.500
and then the report
is fully reimbursed,

00:40:47.500 --> 00:40:49.750
then the AI cost
is diluted on this,

00:40:49.750 --> 00:40:53.530
and that way the government and
the insurers are paying for it.

00:40:53.530 --> 00:40:57.350
If I approach the insurers and
say, pay the API call here,

00:40:57.350 --> 00:40:58.850
they are not going
to understand it.

00:40:58.850 --> 00:41:01.160
But if this is embedded
in the workflow--

00:41:01.160 --> 00:41:03.540
so this is why I say it
cannot be an end in itself,

00:41:03.540 --> 00:41:05.680
it needs to be a means
to deliver something--

00:41:05.680 --> 00:41:07.210
then it's fully reimbursed.

00:41:07.210 --> 00:41:13.130
I do have another case where
AI was important for us.

00:41:13.130 --> 00:41:16.690
We won a bid with the Sao Paolo
public health care last year,

00:41:16.690 --> 00:41:19.120
and so they acquired
the platform.

00:41:19.120 --> 00:41:22.000
And there is a price for AI,
but it's not specifically

00:41:22.000 --> 00:41:24.970
on the bid, say, we're going
to pay x amount for the AI.

00:41:24.970 --> 00:41:27.650
They were asking for features.

00:41:27.650 --> 00:41:30.250
For example, we need
probabilistic matching

00:41:30.250 --> 00:41:32.350
of patients between
different systems,

00:41:32.350 --> 00:41:34.123
because different last names.

00:41:34.123 --> 00:41:36.040
So this is one of the
features that they have.

00:41:36.040 --> 00:41:38.665
We need to be able to
prioritize emergencies

00:41:38.665 --> 00:41:40.540
with machine learning,
was one of the things.

00:41:40.540 --> 00:41:43.660
We need to be able to
audit our previous exams.

00:41:43.660 --> 00:41:46.660
So they have 20 million
exams for the last 10 years

00:41:46.660 --> 00:41:50.590
and now we are running our
models historically on this

00:41:50.590 --> 00:41:55.100
and finding cancers that
was diagnosed as normal.

00:41:55.100 --> 00:41:57.760
And then we can create
clusters and alerts

00:41:57.760 --> 00:41:59.530
and call these patients again.

00:41:59.530 --> 00:42:03.040
So it wasn't like a price,
a tag price on this,

00:42:03.040 --> 00:42:05.860
but being able to have these
features was a difference that

00:42:05.860 --> 00:42:09.010
won the bid, and then the
price is the full platform.

00:42:09.010 --> 00:42:14.940
So it depends how we deliver
and legislation, of course.

00:42:14.940 --> 00:42:17.242
ARIE MEIR: So I'm
hearing that it's

00:42:17.242 --> 00:42:19.700
a complex issue we probably
won't resolve in the next eight

00:42:19.700 --> 00:42:22.515
minutes tonight.

00:42:22.515 --> 00:42:24.140
One thing I want to
ask each one of you

00:42:24.140 --> 00:42:27.260
is what you and your
teams are most excited

00:42:27.260 --> 00:42:28.093
about for this year?

00:42:28.093 --> 00:42:29.385
Like what are you anticipating.

00:42:29.385 --> 00:42:31.202
And what could help
you accelerate?

00:42:37.610 --> 00:42:39.860
RAFAEL DE CASTRO FIGUEROA:
We're excited about it.

00:42:39.860 --> 00:42:41.480
We have many projects
together, Ariel,

00:42:41.480 --> 00:42:43.190
so you already know the answer.

00:42:43.190 --> 00:42:45.080
But like this bit
that I mentioned

00:42:45.080 --> 00:42:48.440
with Sao Paulo public health
care, it's one of the things.

00:42:48.440 --> 00:42:50.390
So what we did in
the private sector

00:42:50.390 --> 00:42:53.640
with these 500 hospitals, it
took six years, one by one,

00:42:53.640 --> 00:42:55.370
bottom up, and now
we have this network

00:42:55.370 --> 00:42:57.200
that is connected
and is running well.

00:42:57.200 --> 00:42:59.300
Now we want to take
this to large scale.

00:42:59.300 --> 00:43:01.840
So we started to work with
the Sao Paulo government.

00:43:01.840 --> 00:43:05.540
And it's a pilot of 35
million citizens, 20 years,

00:43:05.540 --> 00:43:09.480
and when it works we'll then
take this to the federal level.

00:43:09.480 --> 00:43:12.860
So we're now kind of
developing a playbook of how

00:43:12.860 --> 00:43:14.970
to speed this implementation.

00:43:14.970 --> 00:43:16.850
So what takes years
now, for example,

00:43:16.850 --> 00:43:19.760
we take about 18
months to implement

00:43:19.760 --> 00:43:21.470
in a new network in the city.

00:43:21.470 --> 00:43:23.600
If it's a clinic, we
can install in only day.

00:43:23.600 --> 00:43:25.160
Used to take one
week, two weeks,

00:43:25.160 --> 00:43:27.620
and now we start
one clinic per day.

00:43:27.620 --> 00:43:30.590
But now to take this
broadly and faster,

00:43:30.590 --> 00:43:33.750
we want to learn how to do
a full country in 90 days.

00:43:33.750 --> 00:43:36.050
So this is our objective
with Google right now.

00:43:36.050 --> 00:43:39.280
We are by expanding fast
to public health care.

00:43:39.280 --> 00:43:41.200
And it's different
from the private,

00:43:41.200 --> 00:43:42.990
like people are paying for.

00:43:42.990 --> 00:43:44.580
It's this governmental project.

00:43:44.580 --> 00:43:48.200
We need to be very low cost
to be able to be sustainable

00:43:48.200 --> 00:43:50.090
after we deliver the platform.

00:43:50.090 --> 00:43:53.360
So all this cost
engineering is getting

00:43:53.360 --> 00:43:55.280
way more important
right now, because we

00:43:55.280 --> 00:43:57.320
need to deliver a tool
that then is sustainable

00:43:57.320 --> 00:43:58.640
without us taking care.

00:43:58.640 --> 00:44:01.520
And then disconnecting
these health care centers.

00:44:01.520 --> 00:44:05.570
So I think right now we are
just shifting our minds off one

00:44:05.570 --> 00:44:08.975
by one hospitals to a more
broader view of population

00:44:08.975 --> 00:44:11.420
or health care
management, where we

00:44:11.420 --> 00:44:13.580
are applying the same
algorithms that we did one

00:44:13.580 --> 00:44:18.740
by one, on top of way larger and
more heterogeneous data sets.

00:44:18.740 --> 00:44:21.830
And one thing that's
nice is that when

00:44:21.830 --> 00:44:25.310
you compare the metrics that
we have with the top papers

00:44:25.310 --> 00:44:29.000
from MIT, Stanford, we've seen
that the algorithm that they

00:44:29.000 --> 00:44:32.420
train are trained on top
of the Stanford hospital

00:44:32.420 --> 00:44:34.650
with the last GE device.

00:44:34.650 --> 00:44:37.180
And when you try to apply
this in emerging markets data

00:44:37.180 --> 00:44:40.010
sets with different
devices, different DNAs,

00:44:40.010 --> 00:44:41.540
you're not going to generalize.

00:44:41.540 --> 00:44:45.020
You're going to lose a lot of
precision and accuracy on this.

00:44:45.020 --> 00:44:48.250
And we are thrilled to see
that since we trained this

00:44:48.250 --> 00:44:52.160
in different devices in 300
cities from the top [INAUDIBLE]

00:44:52.160 --> 00:44:55.610
Sao Paulo through
lower tier devices,

00:44:55.610 --> 00:44:58.610
for example, a Chinese
device or a Brazilian device,

00:44:58.610 --> 00:45:02.110
and now when you apply these
new data from new hospitals,

00:45:02.110 --> 00:45:03.720
you're generalizing very well.

00:45:03.720 --> 00:45:07.190
So one of the things is that
you need to take the bias out

00:45:07.190 --> 00:45:09.410
of the data sets to be
able to then apply this

00:45:09.410 --> 00:45:11.750
to different
populations, and we see

00:45:11.750 --> 00:45:15.270
that our experience in the
six years was worth it.

00:45:15.270 --> 00:45:18.800
So our goal right now
is to speed up this

00:45:18.800 --> 00:45:23.300
to implement in more countries.

00:45:23.300 --> 00:45:25.970
DANIEL MOLLURA: I'm very
excited about the partnership

00:45:25.970 --> 00:45:30.800
we have for developing an
ecosystem for AI developers

00:45:30.800 --> 00:45:35.390
to interface with the
hospitals where Rad-Aid serves.

00:45:35.390 --> 00:45:38.810
As I said, by building cloud
architecture and building

00:45:38.810 --> 00:45:41.240
capacity in those
hospitals, that

00:45:41.240 --> 00:45:45.860
creates an ability for AI
developers to work with Rad-Aid

00:45:45.860 --> 00:45:49.820
and to work with those hospitals
to bring AI into existence

00:45:49.820 --> 00:45:52.130
for those hospitals.

00:45:52.130 --> 00:45:53.900
And this would be an
open-source system.

00:45:53.900 --> 00:45:55.730
This would be a
way for developers

00:45:55.730 --> 00:45:58.220
to learn more about what
these hospitals need.

00:45:58.220 --> 00:46:00.170
This would increase
data diversity

00:46:00.170 --> 00:46:03.050
by bringing these hospitals
into those repositories that

00:46:03.050 --> 00:46:06.900
really need to be more diverse
for that AI to be accurate.

00:46:06.900 --> 00:46:09.080
So this is an important
endeavor we're

00:46:09.080 --> 00:46:10.370
taking over the next year.

00:46:10.370 --> 00:46:12.320
We invite the AI
development community

00:46:12.320 --> 00:46:15.200
to work with us on
this, to really make

00:46:15.200 --> 00:46:19.850
this a dream come true as an
ecosystem for AI development

00:46:19.850 --> 00:46:21.820
and health care to be together.

00:46:25.263 --> 00:46:27.180
TAKASHI TOCHI: Now we
are proceeding a project

00:46:27.180 --> 00:46:29.700
with the Google Brain team.

00:46:29.700 --> 00:46:33.780
In that project, we are
developing an infrastructure

00:46:33.780 --> 00:46:37.500
to exchange images
and AI results.

00:46:37.500 --> 00:46:41.520
Hospitals and clinics like ours
will send the images to Google.

00:46:41.520 --> 00:46:46.060
And on Google's side, AI scans
and analyzes those images

00:46:46.060 --> 00:46:50.460
and then displays the
result. And very soon

00:46:50.460 --> 00:46:53.380
we can complete it
and show it to you.

00:46:53.380 --> 00:46:59.700
And as Dan mentioned
to PACS vendors,

00:46:59.700 --> 00:47:04.440
I think we should change
the present situation,

00:47:04.440 --> 00:47:09.420
the situation of
vendor-dependent medicine.

00:47:09.420 --> 00:47:16.710
I think we should use AI
like other internet services,

00:47:16.710 --> 00:47:20.130
say, a Google search
when you type a keyword

00:47:20.130 --> 00:47:25.030
and click a button,
and you get the result.

00:47:25.030 --> 00:47:30.770
We can and we should
use AI that way.

00:47:30.770 --> 00:47:35.540
And that infrastructure is
not only for this project,

00:47:35.540 --> 00:47:41.120
but in the near future it would
be one essential part of an AI

00:47:41.120 --> 00:47:42.530
diagnosis system.

00:47:42.530 --> 00:47:46.490
Our work at that infrastructure
can support doctors

00:47:46.490 --> 00:47:48.150
all over the world.

00:47:48.150 --> 00:47:52.300
That's a great honor for us.

00:47:52.300 --> 00:47:55.300
LORENZO RIZZATTO: We are
excited as a company for it.

00:47:55.300 --> 00:48:01.070
We have some new projects on AI
with the regional governments.

00:48:01.070 --> 00:48:03.140
So we are collecting
data from them.

00:48:03.140 --> 00:48:07.440
And then we are
developing with them

00:48:07.440 --> 00:48:10.600
some artificial
intelligence tools.

00:48:10.600 --> 00:48:17.740
And so, of course, it's really
exciting for the company.

00:48:20.470 --> 00:48:23.150
ARIE MEIR: So I just want to
add on what Takashi and Dan said

00:48:23.150 --> 00:48:24.150
on the ecosystem.

00:48:24.150 --> 00:48:26.560
We definitely take an
ecosystem approach.

00:48:26.560 --> 00:48:29.280
So our view from
Google Cloud, we

00:48:29.280 --> 00:48:32.130
want to enable
application developers

00:48:32.130 --> 00:48:35.070
to build AI applications in
health care and radiology

00:48:35.070 --> 00:48:37.380
generally, because
these applications tend

00:48:37.380 --> 00:48:38.347
to be very narrow.

00:48:38.347 --> 00:48:39.930
And it's going to
take hundreds, maybe

00:48:39.930 --> 00:48:41.220
thousands of applications.

00:48:41.220 --> 00:48:43.530
We believe that it will
take an entire ecosystem

00:48:43.530 --> 00:48:46.140
to develop this effectively.

00:48:46.140 --> 00:48:48.640
We invest where we can
accelerate this process.

00:48:48.640 --> 00:48:50.790
For example, how to develop
better labeling tools

00:48:50.790 --> 00:48:52.680
or how to develop
better training tools.

00:48:52.680 --> 00:48:54.180
But the application
themselves would

00:48:54.180 --> 00:48:57.240
be developed by the ecosystem.

00:48:57.240 --> 00:48:59.310
We have a minute
left, and so I want

00:48:59.310 --> 00:49:01.450
to ask you in 10
seconds, what's going

00:49:01.450 --> 00:49:03.810
to be your prediction
for the next 18 months?

00:49:03.810 --> 00:49:05.630
Lightning round.

00:49:05.630 --> 00:49:07.100
Who wants to go first?

00:49:11.510 --> 00:49:13.550
RAFAEL DE CASTRO FIGUEROA: OK.

00:49:13.550 --> 00:49:15.470
Actually, my prediction
is the challenge

00:49:15.470 --> 00:49:16.500
that we need to solve.

00:49:16.500 --> 00:49:19.340
So I hope we fulfill
this prophecy.

00:49:19.340 --> 00:49:21.980
But right now these
models are very narrow.

00:49:21.980 --> 00:49:23.020
So it's the same.

00:49:23.020 --> 00:49:24.310
Pneumonia, yes or no?

00:49:24.310 --> 00:49:25.820
Pneumothorax, yes or no?

00:49:25.820 --> 00:49:30.230
What we're trying to break now
is correlating this with a lab

00:49:30.230 --> 00:49:33.290
test, for example, with EHR,
and have a deep learning

00:49:33.290 --> 00:49:36.020
model to actually
look at multiple signs

00:49:36.020 --> 00:49:37.970
to then do a proper
telediagnostic,

00:49:37.970 --> 00:49:40.230
because we don't believe
that to only look at an image

00:49:40.230 --> 00:49:42.390
is enough even for a doctor.

00:49:42.390 --> 00:49:43.927
So imagine the AI
that don't have

00:49:43.927 --> 00:49:46.010
any knowledge of the
[? word ?] making predictions

00:49:46.010 --> 00:49:48.230
on top of pixels only.

00:49:48.230 --> 00:49:50.870
So we're trying to
cross these barriers

00:49:50.870 --> 00:49:54.070
and create multi-modality models
that absorb all kinds of health

00:49:54.070 --> 00:49:56.317
care signals.

00:49:56.317 --> 00:49:58.650
DANIEL MOLLURA: I think
there'll be a full embrace of AI

00:49:58.650 --> 00:50:03.097
education in the clinical world
that in the next 18 months,

00:50:03.097 --> 00:50:05.430
you're going to see a lot of
other medical professionals

00:50:05.430 --> 00:50:09.210
fully embracing AI education
in order to prepare themselves

00:50:09.210 --> 00:50:14.120
for the next era where AI
will come into practice.

00:50:14.120 --> 00:50:18.940
TAKASHI TOCHI: In this event,
Google Next, in every session,

00:50:18.940 --> 00:50:22.510
in every session related to
health care, every speaker,

00:50:22.510 --> 00:50:24.850
every panelist says
the same thing,

00:50:24.850 --> 00:50:28.630
talking about the problem
of interoperability,

00:50:28.630 --> 00:50:29.560
that kind of thing.

00:50:29.560 --> 00:50:32.320
To break down barriers.

00:50:32.320 --> 00:50:39.170
I think-- and now I'm very
optimistic in the long run.

00:50:39.170 --> 00:50:46.090
And I think this change
can be a transition

00:50:46.090 --> 00:50:53.590
from paternalistic medicine
to patient-centered medicine.

00:50:53.590 --> 00:50:57.070
And that will be
beneficial for patients.

00:50:57.070 --> 00:50:58.000
Thank you.

00:50:58.000 --> 00:51:00.910
LORENZO RIZZATTO: I see
that standardization also,

00:51:00.910 --> 00:51:05.090
in artificial intelligence, will
become more and more important.

00:51:05.090 --> 00:51:11.590
So I'm sure Google Cloud will
lead the way in data labeling,

00:51:11.590 --> 00:51:15.915
for example, or implementation
of [INAUDIBLE] annotation

00:51:15.915 --> 00:51:18.050
in [? the viewers. ?]
This is really important,

00:51:18.050 --> 00:51:24.040
and everyone is doing
some private annotation,

00:51:24.040 --> 00:51:27.700
or some private data labeling,
but this cannot continue.

